    "q-105397": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105397",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The magnitude of a force $F$ applied to an object is related to the work $W$ and the distance $d$ by the formula $F=\\frac{W}{d}.$  Given that $W$ is measured in $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}$ and the distance is measured in meters, determine an appropriate measurement unit for the force.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the unit of the force, we substitute the given units of work and distance into the formula and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\frac{W}{d} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{m}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
